 "phrases": [
  "Your microwave does not cook food from the inside out. That's a myth.",
  "So what's really happening in there? Let's look.",
  "On the side, there's a tube called a magnetron.",
  "It fires invisible waves. The same kind your Wi-Fi uses.",
  "They bounce off the metal walls, again and again.",
  "Now zoom into your food. Into a single drop of water.",
  "Water molecules are lopsided. One side slightly positive, the other slightly negative.",
  "The waves flip back and forth, about two and a half billion times a second.",
  "Each flip, the molecules try to turn and follow. They jostle their neighbors.",
  "That jostling is heat.",
  "But the waves only get into the outer layer of your food.",
  "The center warms up later, slowly, from the outside in.",
  "And the waves don't spread evenly. They leave hot spots and cold spots.",
  "That's why the plate spins. And why you should stir halfway.",
  "Last thing. Look at the door. Those holes are smaller than the waves. Light gets out. Microwaves don't.",
  "Pop quiz: why does frozen food heat so unevenly? Comment your answer.",
  "Follow Tech Unseen. Next lesson: how does GPS know exactly where you are?"
 ],
